Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Sun Sep 20 04:17:40 2026 -0500
Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Sun Sep 20 04:17:40 2026 -0500

    fix(api): queue list newest-first + honest total (DF-OFF-BY-ONE-10)
    
    GET /api/v1/queue answered the wrong question twice over.
    
    Defect A — order. List scanned `ORDER BY priority DESC, created_at ASC`, so
    historical rows imported with a priority above a live POST-submit score stayed
    pinned to page 1 forever; with thousands of pending rows in the DB a live
    listing returned complete/failed history and zero pending. List is the
    USER-FACING view ("what did I submit / what is waiting"), so it now scans
    `ORDER BY created_at DESC, id ASC` — newest submission first. The solver's
    scheduling order is untouched: pickPending keeps `priority DESC, created_at
    ASC`, and the new Queue.PendingQueueOrder exposes that same order for the only
    callers that need a pending place (Server.pendingPositions, Server.queuePosition).
    
    Defect B — total. handleListQueue set Total = len(entries), i.e. the page size
    clamped at limit, so a caller could not tell "2 000 jobs waiting" from "100
    returned, no more". Queue.ListPage (List delegates to it, signature unchanged)
    now also returns the size of the unpaginated match set — after the status
    filter AND after placeholder-class exclusion — and the handler publishes it.
    An offset past the end still reports the real match count.
    
    Because position/estimated_time were derived from List's order, they would have
    silently inverted with the listing; pendingPositions and queuePosition now read
    PendingQueueOrder, so a pending entry still reports the jobs actually ahead of
    it (the trap the brief flagged). Verified identical pre-fix vs post-fix on the
    same DB: position 1 / estimated_time 3m12s.
    
    Regression coverage (all RED-proven by reverting the fix, then restored):
    - internal/ingest TestQueue_List_NewestFirstAndHonestTotal — old high-priority
      row vs new low-priority row; asserts page 1 leads with the new row, that the
      match count survives paging and offset-past-end, and that the scheduler order
      still ranks the old high-priority row first.
    - internal/ingest TestQueue_ListPage_TotalExcludesPlaceholdersAndCountsMatches —
      count is post-status-filter and post-placeholder, independent of page size.
    - internal/api TestListQueue_NewestFirstHonestTotalAndPendingPosition — full
      listing order, limit=1 over 5 matches (len==1, total==5), offset paging,
      status=pending, and the pending-place contract (position follows solver
      order, not the listing index; terminal rows keep position 0 / empty ETA).
    - internal/api TestListQueue_PositionAndEstimatedTime: ETA expectations now
      derive from PendingQueueOrder, not from the listing's index.
    - pkg/api TestOpenAPISpec_ParametersCarryContinuationKeys: parameter count
      15 -> 17 (GET /api/v1/queue now documents its limit/offset pair).
    
    Docs: api-reference.md documents newest-first ordering and that `total` is the
    match count, not the page size; openapi.yaml gains the queue operation
    description, the two query parameters, and the `total` semantics.
    
    Live A/B on a scratch copy of off-by-one.db with a pending row on top, both
    binaries against the same DB — prefix dc69717 on :8769: total 100, entries[0]
    = sub_3c29e6, index of sub_judge1 null; post-fix on :8768: total 4010,
    entries[0] = sub_judge1, index 0; `status=pending` -> total 1, [sub_judge1];
    detail position 1 / 3m12s on both.
    
    go build ./... && go vet ./... && gofmt -l cmd/ internal/ pkg/ sql/ clean;
    go test ./... -short -p 1 -count=1 -timeout 300s all ok.
    
